## CI setup and upgrades

The initial workflow files are manual-only configuration stubs. After PNNL
publishes an `openstudio-si` release containing plugin development tools and
reviews the shared workflows, activate them with:

```bash
openstudio-si plugin configure-ci . \
  --workflow-ref <full-PNNL-workflow-commit-SHA> \
  --tooling-version <published-openstudio-si-version>
```

The command installs no packages. It records the exact stable PyPI version and
writes thin calls to the pinned PNNL workflows. The version defaults to the
installed `openstudio-si` version (minimum 0.4.3). CI probes the required
commands before proceeding. Run it again with a newer ref/version to
upgrade without recreating this project. Edited managed workflows require
reconciliation before upgrading. Commit the resulting files.

The read-only build installs `openstudio-si[plugin-dev]==<version>` from PyPI
in a clean environment with binary wheels. Dependencies resolve for the selected
platform/Python; this pins the tool release, not every transitive dependency.
Checkout uses persist-credentials: false. The separate upload-only job has
contents: write, performs no checkout or dependency installation, and uploads
candidate artifacts. No model credentials or LLM calls are used.

## Identity, license and evaluation

Review owner, maintainers and dependencies in plugin/openstudio-si-plugin.yaml.
The license was explicitly selected during generation, and its copyright holder
uses the supplied holder or organization/maintainer. The template does not decide
an approved default for a lab; confirm the choice with its legal/IP office.

The manifest version is also the Python build version. Hatch reads it using its
[regex version source](https://hatch.pypa.io/latest/plugins/version-source/regex/).
Keep schema/API ranges separate from plugin release numbers. Evaluation case and
run configuration are draft 0.1. The evaluation runner arrives in step C; no eval
command or extra is claimed available yet. Judge scores remain advisory.

## Marketplace release

Before tagging, run plugin check-project . --require-tracked locally. CI cannot
recover ignored files that never reached Git. The same check in a fresh CI
checkout is only a consistency check.

After CI setup, publish a source GitHub release with tag v<manifest-version>.
The shared build validates tracked source, checks hosts, verifies the tag, and
creates reproducible archives plus hashes/source provenance. The upload-only job
stages those assets on this repository's release. Re-runs use --clobber so a
partial earlier upload can be completed. The intended destination is a
plugin-scoped PR in pnnl/openstudio-si-plugins, using the central importer.
That automatic submission is step E and is explicitly still pending. There is
no lab PyPI release and no shared-marketplace write token in the build job.

Generation provenance is in .openstudio-si-template.json; CI setup is recorded
in .openstudio-si/ci.json (workflow commit and tool release).

The SDK is installed with `openstudio-si[plugin-dev]`, but generation and
packaging do not import it or require a native OpenStudio CLI. Budget roughly
370 MB for the environment; size varies with platform and dependencies. The
pinned OpenStudio 3.11.0 package currently has no Windows Python 3.14 wheel;
use a supported Python/platform combination (CI uses Linux/Python 3.13).
